//? Title: Multidimensional Scaling: Recovering Geometry from Distances
//? Subtitle: Double-centring, strain versus distance error, non-Euclidean spectra and the exact PCA connection, with datamunge's stats::MDS
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given only the pairwise dissimilarities between objects, we want coordinates for a map that
shows their relationships. A two-dimensional map generally cannot preserve every distance,
and some dissimilarities cannot be represented exactly in any Euclidean dimension.
Classical multidimensional scaling recovers a centred inner-product matrix from squared
distances and retains its leading positive eigenpairs. We derive this construction and its
least-squares guarantee, which concerns inner products rather than distances. A four-point
rectangle makes every centring step checkable; a four-cycle shows why retaining all positive
inertia can still leave distance error. An analytic spectrum sweep checks where Euclidean
representability fails. Finally, iris illustrates dimensionality choice, Euclidean versus
Manhattan dissimilarities, and the exact equivalence with centred, unscaled PCA. On iris, two Euclidean axes retain 97.77% of positive inertia, while the relative
distance error is 4.18%; the Manhattan map has a larger 5.79% distance error and a
substantial negative spectrum. Labels are used only to colour the resulting map.
)

\toc

> The method

>> What is observed, and what must be reconstructed?

Let \(D=(d_{ij})\) be a symmetric, non-negative dissimilarity matrix on \(n\) objects,
with \(d_{ii}=0\). We seek points \(y_i\in\mathbb R^k\) whose Euclidean distances
\(\hat d_{ij}=\lVert y_i-y_j\rVert\) resemble \(d_{ij}\).
The input may come from measured distances without feature vectors, or from a metric on
observed features. `MDS::from_distances(D,k)` accepts the former; the DataFrame constructor
builds Euclidean or Manhattan distances from selected numeric columns. Neither path
standardises features or makes a non-Euclidean matrix Euclidean by adding a constant.

The problem has an unavoidable ambiguity: translation, rotation and reflection preserve all
distances. Repeated eigenvalues also permit arbitrary choices of basis within their
subspace. Comparing coordinates one by one is therefore usually the wrong verification.
Compare pair distances or the centred Gram matrix instead.

>> Distances determine a centred Gram matrix

If rows of \(X\) are Euclidean coordinates, write \(G=XX^\top\) and
\(q_i=\lVert x_i\rVert^2\). Squared distances satisfy

$$
D^{(2)}=q\mathbf1^\top+\mathbf1q^\top-2XX^\top,
\qquad J=I-\frac1n\mathbf1\mathbf1^\top.
$$
\alttext(The matrix of squared distances is the vector of squared point norms repeated across rows, plus its transpose, minus twice X X transpose. J is the identity minus the all-ones matrix divided by n.)

Since \(J\mathbf1=0\), multiplying on both sides by \(J\) eliminates the norm terms:

$$
B=-\tfrac12JD^{(2)}J=(JX)(JX)^\top,
\qquad
B_{ij}=-\tfrac12\left(d_{ij}^2-\overline{d^2}_{i\cdot}
-\overline{d^2}_{\cdot j}+\overline{d^2}_{\cdot\cdot}\right).
$$
\alttext(B is minus one half J times the squared-distance matrix times J. For Euclidean input this equals the Gram matrix of centred coordinates. Each entry subtracts the squared-distance row and column means and adds their grand mean before multiplying by minus one half.)

`gram_matrix()` exposes this intermediate matrix, not the truncated reconstruction. Its
row sums vanish up to round-off. For Euclidean input it is positive semidefinite (PSD):
\(z^\top Bz=\lVert X^\top Jz\rVert^2\geq0\).
Conversely, if the double-centred \(B\) is PSD, factor it as \(YY^\top\); the corresponding
squared distances reproduce the original hollow symmetric \(D^{(2)}\). To see the last
claim, expand \(B_{ii}+B_{jj}-2B_{ij}\): all centring means cancel, leaving \(d_{ij}^2\).
Thus non-negative Gram eigenvalues characterise exact Euclidean representability.

>> The eigenvalue solution and what it optimises

Let \(B=V\Lambda V^\top\), with eigenvalues in descending order. Classical MDS takes

$$
Y_k=V_{:,1:k}\operatorname{diag}\left(\sqrt{\max(\lambda_1,0)},\ldots,
\sqrt{\max(\lambda_k,0)}\right).
$$
\alttext(The k-dimensional coordinates are the leading k eigenvectors, with each column multiplied by the square root of its eigenvalue clamped below at zero.)

Its guarantee is about *strain*, the squared Frobenius error in the Gram matrix:

$$
\min_{Y\in\mathbb R^{n\times k}}\lVert B-YY^\top\rVert_F^2
=\sum_{j>k,\lambda_j>0}\lambda_j^2+\sum_{\lambda_j<0}\lambda_j^2.
$$
\alttext(The least squared Gram error over k-dimensional coordinates equals the sum of squared omitted positive eigenvalues plus the sum of all squared negative eigenvalues.)

A proof sketch separates eigenvalues from orientation. The eigenvalues of \(YY^\top\)
are non-negative with at most \(k\) non-zero entries. Expanding the squared error gives
\(\operatorname{tr}(B^2)+\operatorname{tr}(A^2)-2\operatorname{tr}(BA)\), with
\(A=YY^\top\). Aligning their largest eigenvalues maximises the cross term; minimising
each remaining \((\lambda_j-a_j)^2\), subject to \(a_j\geq0\), retains the largest
positive \(\lambda_j\) and sets the others to zero. Negative eigenvalues therefore leave
an irreducible strain floor in every Euclidean embedding.

This is not a guarantee of minimum squared error in distances. Two useful distance
summaries, reported by `dimension_diagnostics()`, are

$$
\mathrm{RMSE}_k=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i<j}(\hat d_{ij,k}-d_{ij})^2}{\binom n2}},
\qquad
E_k=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i<j}(\hat d_{ij,k}-d_{ij})^2}{\sum_{i<j}d_{ij}^2}}.
$$
\alttext(Distance RMSE is the square root of the mean squared pairwise distance residual. Relative distance error E is the square root of the sum of squared residuals divided by the sum of squared input dissimilarities.)

The second column is named `relative_distance_error`: its denominator uses the input
matrix, explicitly fixing the normalisation. We do not label it Kruskal Stress-1, whose
normalisation can use fitted distances. Both summaries are computed over unordered pairs.
`pair_distances()` exposes every residual; `plot_shepard()` plots \(\hat d\) against \(d\).
On Euclidean input, truncated distances never exceed the originals and increase with added
axes, so these distance errors decrease. That monotonicity need not hold for indefinite input.

>> Two goodness-of-fit fractions

The original `goodness_of_fit()` reports retained positive inertia. For comparison with
R's `cmdscale`, the diagnostics expose both of its spectral fractions:

$$
G_+(k)=\frac{\sum_{j\leq k}\max(\lambda_j,0)}{\sum_j\max(\lambda_j,0)},
\qquad
G_{|\cdot|}(k)=\frac{\sum_{j\leq k}\max(\lambda_j,0)}{\sum_j|\lambda_j|}.
$$
\alttext(G positive divides the sum of retained positive eigenvalues by the total positive eigenvalue sum. G absolute divides the same numerator by the sum of absolute eigenvalues.)

For PSD input these coincide. Otherwise \(G_+=1\) says only that every positive direction
was retained: it ignores the negative spectrum. Neither fraction is a percentage of distances
reproduced, nor a percentage of the strain removed. `relative_strain` instead reports the
square root of the residual eigenvalue-square sum divided by \(\sum_j\lambda_j^2\).
The all-zero matrix has no inertia; the implementation reports zero for its fit fractions
and zero for its distortion measures. Tiny eigenvalues of either sign can be round-off;
`spectrum()` flags a negative value only below \(-10^{-10}\max_j|\lambda_j|\).

>> The exact relationship to PCA

For Euclidean feature distances, centre \(X\) and write its singular value decomposition
as \(X_c=U\Sigma V^\top\). Then

$$
B=X_cX_c^\top=U\Sigma^2U^\top,\qquad
\frac{X_c^\top X_c}{n-1}=V\frac{\Sigma^2}{n-1}V^\top,
\qquad Y_k=U_k\Sigma_k=X_cV_k.
$$
\alttext(The centred Gram matrix has eigenvalues equal to squared singular values. The sample covariance has those eigenvalues divided by n minus one. MDS coordinates and unscaled PCA scores both equal U k times Sigma k.)

Thus the non-zero MDS eigenvalues are exactly \((n-1)\) times the PCA covariance
eigenvalues; their positive-inertia proportions agree. The coordinates agree up to
orthogonal choices of axes. This is an equality, not an approximate correlation claim.
The comparison must use `PCAOptions{true,false}`: PCA centres and scales by default,
whereas the feature MDS constructor uses the original units. Scaling changes the question.

>> Computation and limits

Feature distances cost \(O(n^2p)\) work, double-centring costs \(O(n^2)\), and dense
storage costs \(O(n^2)\). The implementation uses cyclic Jacobi eigendecomposition,
with \(O(n^3)\) work per sweep and a relative off-diagonal tolerance of \(10^{-12}\)
(up to 100 sweeps). All \(n\) eigenvalues are retained for diagnostics. Prefix diagnostics
reuse the fitted axes in \(O(n^2k)\) work, rather than refitting \(k\) models.
This is classical scaling, not iterative stress minimisation or non-metric MDS.
There is no out-of-sample transform in this API. Coordinates have the input distance units;
plots use their canvas aspect ratio and should not be used as rulers.

> Simple examples

>> A 4 by 3 rectangle: recover the hidden coordinates

Take \(A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,3),D=(0,3)\). The squared-distance matrix has rows
\((0,16,25,9)\), \((16,0,9,25)\), \((25,9,0,16)\), \((9,25,16,0)\).
Every row mean and the grand mean equal \(50/4=12.5\). Consequently
\(B_{AA}=-\tfrac12(0-12.5)=6.25\) and
\(B_{AB}=-\tfrac12(16-12.5)=-1.75\). The entire intermediate matrix is printed below.
Its two non-zero eigenvalues are 16 and 9, so two dimensions recover all distances.
The axes may change sign, but the reconstructed rectangle is the same geometry.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mds_rectangle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data, gram;
  data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,4,4,0});
  data.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,0,3,3});
  const dm::stats::MDS model(data,{"x","y"});
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"A","B","C","D"};
  gram.add_column("row",labels);
  for (std::size_t j=0;j<4;++j) gram.add_column(labels[j],model.gram_matrix().col(j));
  auto plot=model.plot_embedding();
  const auto x=model.dimension(0), y=model.dimension(1);
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i) {
    const auto j=(i+1)%4;
    plot.segments({x[i]},{y[i]},{x[j]},{y[j]},{90,90,90},1.2);
    plot.text(x[i]+0.18,y[i]+0.18,labels[i]);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.title("Recovered 4 by 3 rectangle").x_limits(-3,3).y_limits(-2.25,2.25)
      .size(720,611).save_svg("build/mds_rectangle.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",gram.to_md(2));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| row | A | B | C | D |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| A | 6.25 | -1.75 | -6.25 | 1.75 |
| B | -1.75 | 6.25 | 1.75 | -6.25 |
| C | -6.25 | 1.75 | 6.25 | -1.75 |
| D | 1.75 | -6.25 | -1.75 | 6.25 |

\image(figures/mds_rectangle.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The double-centred Gram matrix and recovered rectangle. B has rank two; the coordinate origin is the centroid, not vertex A.)
\alttext(Four labelled points form a rectangle centred on the origin, with horizontal coordinates minus and plus two and vertical coordinates minus and plus one and a half. Grey edges join A to B to C to D and back to A.)

>> Dropping an axis: the exact strain and the actual distance loss

Keeping only the eigenvalue 16 discards 9, hence \(G_+(1)=16/25=0.64\) but the
squared Gram error is \(9^2=81\), not 36% of a distance loss. Vertices sharing the
same horizontal coordinate coincide in one dimension: the vertical sides shrink from 3
to 0 and the diagonals from 5 to 4. Horizontal sides keep length 4. Therefore

$$
\mathrm{RMSE}_1=\sqrt{\frac{2(3-0)^2+2(5-4)^2}{6}}=\sqrt{10/3},
\qquad E_1=\sqrt{\frac{20}{100}}=1/\sqrt5.
$$
\alttext(The one-dimensional distance RMSE is the square root of twenty divided by six, or ten thirds. The relative distance error is the square root of twenty divided by one hundred, or one over square root five.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mds_rectangle_shepard.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data;
  data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,4,4,0});
  data.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,0,3,3});
  const dm::stats::MDS model(data,{"x","y"});
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  layout.add(model.plot_shepard(1).title("One axis: vertical sides collapse"))
        .add(model.plot_shepard(2).title("Two axes: exact distances"));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,480).fit_panels().save_svg("build/mds_rectangle_shepard.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",model.dimension_diagnostics().to_md(6));
}
```
