| dimensions | gof_positive | gof_absolute | strain_squared | relative_strain | distance_rmse | relative_distance_error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.64 | 0.64 | 81 | 0.490261 | 1.825742 | 0.447214 |
| 2 | 1.00 | 1.00 | 0 | 0.000000 | 0.000000 | 0.000000 |

\image(figures/mds_rectangle_shepard.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Prefix diagnostics and Shepard plots for the rectangle. The grey equality line is exact distance preservation; overlapping pairs reduce the six observations to three visible points.)
\alttext(Two distance-versus-distance panels. With one axis, the points are at input and fitted distances 3 and 0, 4 and 4, and 5 and 4. With two axes they lie on the equality line at 3, 4 and 5.)

>> A four-cycle: perfect positive inertia is not perfect distance preservation

Consider four objects around a cycle. Adjacent dissimilarities are 1; opposite ones are 2.
These are the Manhattan distances between the corners of a unit square. They obey the
triangle inequality but cannot be Euclidean distances with the same values. The full
Gram spectrum is \((2,2,0,-1)\). Classical scaling drops the negative direction and
returns a square of side \(\sqrt2\) and diagonal 2. It retains every positive eigenvalue,
yet makes all four sides too long. The exact diagnostics are

$$
G_+(2)=1,\quad G_{|\cdot|}(2)=4/5,\quad
\lVert B-Y_2Y_2^\top\rVert_F^2=1,\quad
E_2=\frac{\sqrt2-1}{\sqrt3}.
$$
\alttext(The positive-inertia goodness of fit is one, the absolute-inertia fraction is four fifths, squared strain is one, and relative distance error is square root two minus one divided by square root three.)

There is an explicit configuration with *smaller distance loss*. A square of side \(s\)
has squared distance loss \(4(s-1)^2+2(\sqrt2s-2)^2\). Differentiating gives
\(16s-8-8\sqrt2=0\), so \(s=(1+\sqrt2)/2\). Its loss is half that of the classical
solution. This candidate suffices to disprove distance-loss optimality; we do not claim
it is the optimum over all possible four-point configurations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mds_cycle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> d(4,4,{0,1,2,1, 1,0,1,2, 2,1,0,1, 1,2,1,0});
  const auto model=dm::stats::MDS::from_distances(d,3);
  const auto pairs=model.pair_distances(2);
  const double side=(1+std::sqrt(2.0))/2;
  auto loss=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  loss.points(pairs.numeric_column("dissimilarity").values(),pairs.numeric_column("embedded_distance").values(),
              "Classical",{37,99,235},6)
      .points({1,2},{side,std::sqrt(2.0)*side},"Lower distance loss",{220,38,38},6)
      .segments({0},{0},{2.8},{2.8},{90,90,90},1)
      .x_limits(0,2.8).y_limits(0,2.8).x_label("Input dissimilarity").y_label("Embedded distance")
      .title("Two different least-squares targets");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  layout.add(model.plot_embedding().title("Classical two-axis map").x_limits(-1.9,1.9).y_limits(-1.5,1.5))
        .add(loss);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/mds_cycle.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n",model.spectrum().select({"dimension","eigenvalue","significant_negative"}).to_md(3),
             model.dimension_diagnostics().to_md(6));
}
```
