| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.5 | 0.4 | 5 | 0.745356 | 0.806459 | 0.570252 |
| 2 | 1.0 | 0.8 | 1 | 0.333333 | 0.338204 | 0.239146 |
| 3 | 1.0 | 0.8 | 1 | 0.333333 | 0.338204 | 0.239146 |

\image(figures/mds_cycle.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The negative eigenvalue leaves a strain floor even with three requested axes. In the right panel the red square configuration has half the blue configuration's summed squared distance loss.)
\alttext(Left: four points form a square centred on the origin. Right: blue classical distances are about 1.414 for adjacent objects and 2 for opposite objects. Red alternative distances are about 1.207 and 1.707. A grey equality line makes the trade-off visible.)

> Checking the theory

>> Sweep through the boundary of Euclidean representability

Generalise the preceding cycle so opposite dissimilarities are \(t\), while adjacent ones
remain 1. For \(1\leq t\leq2\) these still form a metric. Its centred Gram eigenvectors
can be chosen proportional to \((1,0,-1,0)\), \((0,1,0,-1)\),
\((1,-1,1,-1)\) and \((1,1,1,1)\). Their eigenvalues are

$$
\{t^2/2,t^2/2,1-t^2/2,0\},\qquad
\text{irreducible squared strain}=\left[\max(0,t^2/2-1)\right]^2.
$$
\alttext(The four eigenvalues are t squared over two twice, one minus t squared over two, and zero. The irreducible squared strain is the square of the positive part of t squared over two minus one.)

The third direction is positive below \(\sqrt2\), zero at \(\sqrt2\), and negative
above it. Three dimensions exactly represent the first regime; two suffice at the boundary;
no dimension can exactly represent the final regime. We check the complete spectrum and
strain floor at 101 values of \(t\). Errors compare sorted spectra, so eigenvector
non-uniqueness cannot disguise a failure.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mds_cycle_sweep.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
int main() {
  std::vector<double> t,positive,changing,floor,error;
  double max_eigen_error=0,max_floor_error=0;
  for (int i=0;i<=100;++i) {
    const double a=1+i/100.0;
    const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> d(4,4,{0,1,a,1, 1,0,1,a, a,1,0,1, 1,a,1,0});
    const auto model=dm::stats::MDS::from_distances(d,3);
    const auto path=model.dimension_diagnostics();
    std::vector<double> exact{a*a/2,a*a/2,1-a*a/2,0};
    std::sort(exact.begin(),exact.end(),std::greater<double>());
    for (std::size_t j=0;j<4;++j) max_eigen_error=std::max(max_eigen_error,std::abs(exact[j]-model.eigenvalues()[j]));
    const double predicted=std::pow(std::max(0.0,a*a/2-1),2);
    max_floor_error=std::max(max_floor_error,std::abs(predicted-path.double_at("strain_squared",2)));
    t.push_back(a); positive.push_back(a*a/2); changing.push_back(1-a*a/2);
    floor.push_back(path.double_at("relative_strain",2)); error.push_back(path.double_at("relative_distance_error",2));
  }
  auto eigen=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), residual=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  eigen.lines(t,positive,"Repeated eigenvalue",{37,99,235}).lines(t,changing,"Changing eigenvalue",{220,38,38})
       .abline_h(0,{90,90,90}).abline_v(std::sqrt(2.0),{90,90,90})
       .title("An eigenvalue crosses zero").x_label("Opposite dissimilarity t").y_label("Gram eigenvalue").y_limits(-1.2,3.2);
  residual.lines(t,floor,"Gram error",{37,99,235}).lines(t,error,"Distance error",{220,38,38})
       .abline_v(std::sqrt(2.0),{90,90,90}).title("A negative mode leaves a residual")
       .x_label("Opposite dissimilarity t").y_label("Relative residual").y_limits(-0.02,0.55);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(eigen).add(residual);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/mds_cycle_sweep.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame checks;
  checks.add_column("maximum eigenvalue error",std::vector<double>{max_eigen_error});
  checks.add_column("maximum strain-floor error",std::vector<double>{max_floor_error});
  std::print("{}\n",checks.to_md(12));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| maximum eigenvalue error | maximum strain-floor error |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 |

\image(figures/mds_cycle_sweep.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The closed-form eigenvalues and the observed three-axis residuals across the Euclidean/non-Euclidean boundary. The error table checks every fitted spectrum and strain floor.)
\alttext(Left: a blue eigenvalue curve rises from 0.5 to 2; a red curve falls from 0.5 to minus 1, crossing zero at t equal to square root two. Right: both residuals are effectively zero before this crossing and increase afterwards. Relative strain reaches one third at t equal to two.)

> A dataset example: iris

>> Four measurements, with species withheld from the fit

`dm::datasets::iris()` contains 150 flowers and four measurements in centimetres, plus
species. No selected feature is missing here. More generally, the constructor drops rows
with a null selected feature; `kept_row_indices()` must then be used to align external labels.
The species means below describe the input but are never supplied to MDS as predictors.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  std::print("{} flowers, {} columns.\n\n",iris.nrows(),iris.ncols());
  std::print("{}\n",iris | dm::group_by("Species")
      | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("Sepal.Length","Sepal length"),
                      dm::agg::mean("Sepal.Width","Sepal width"),
                      dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length","Petal length"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Width","Petal width"))
      | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
150 flowers, 5 columns.

| Species | n | Sepal length | Sepal width | Petal length | Petal width |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 50 | 5.006 | 3.428 | 1.462 | 0.246 |
| versicolor | 50 | 5.936 | 2.770 | 4.260 | 1.326 |
| virginica | 50 | 6.588 | 2.974 | 5.552 | 2.026 |
// result_end
\caption(Class sizes and mean measurements in centimetres. All four numeric columns are used; species is used only for description and plot colours.)

>> Euclidean and Manhattan maps answer different questions

Both maps below use raw centimetres. Euclidean distance is the straight-line length in
four-dimensional measurement space; Manhattan distance adds absolute differences in each
measurement. Squaring the latter does not generally produce a PSD centred Gram matrix.
Consequently its positive-inertia fraction alone cannot describe map quality.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mds_iris_maps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> features{"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  std::vector<std::string> names; std::vector<double> gof,absolute,relative_error,negative;
  for (auto metric : {dm::stats::DistanceMetric::Euclidean,dm::stats::DistanceMetric::Manhattan}) {
    const dm::stats::MDS model(iris,features,{2,metric});
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    for (auto row : model.kept_row_indices()) labels.push_back(iris.string_at("Species",row));
    const auto diag=model.dimension_diagnostics();
    names.push_back(metric==dm::stats::DistanceMetric::Euclidean ? "Euclidean" : "Manhattan");
    gof.push_back(diag.double_at("gof_positive",1)); absolute.push_back(diag.double_at("gof_absolute",1));
    relative_error.push_back(diag.double_at("relative_distance_error",1));
    const auto flags=model.spectrum().numeric_column("significant_negative").values();
    negative.push_back(std::accumulate(flags.begin(),flags.end(),0.0));
    layout.add(model.plot_embedding(labels).title(names.back()).y_limits(-3,4));
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,510).fit_panels().save_svg("build/mds_iris_maps.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("metric",names); table.add_column("positive inertia",gof); table.add_column("absolute inertia",absolute);
  table.add_column("relative distance error",relative_error); table.add_column("negative modes",negative);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| metric | positive inertia | absolute inertia | relative distance error | negative modes |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Euclidean | 0.977685 | 0.977685 | 0.041796 | 0 |
| Manhattan | 0.894589 | 0.812986 | 0.057863 | 92 |

\image(figures/mds_iris_maps.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two-dimensional iris maps, fitted without species. Negative-mode counts use the relative spectral tolerance; the small negative eigenvalues of a Euclidean Gram matrix are numerical round-off.)
\alttext(Two panels show the same flowers coloured by species after Euclidean and Manhattan scaling. Setosa forms a separate cluster at one end of the first axis, while versicolor and virginica overlap. The different metrics change the spread and placement of points.)

For these two-axis fits the Euclidean relative distance error is 4.18%, while the Manhattan
value is 5.79%. The Manhattan positive-inertia fraction is 89.46%, but its absolute-inertia
fraction is only 81.30%; 92 eigenvalues are negative beyond the stated tolerance. That
difference explains why its positive-inertia fraction needs a separate residual check.

A map can reveal separation without being a classifier. No supervised accuracy is estimated
here, and these in-sample distance diagnostics do not measure prediction on new flowers.
The colours interpret an unsupervised result; they did not guide its construction.

>> How much is lost by keeping two dimensions?

Four dimensions suffice for the Euclidean distances of four-feature rows. Fitting four
axes once lets the library inspect every prefix. The positive-inertia proportion and
normalised strain differ because one weights eigenvalues and the other their squares.
Actual distance errors add a third, directly interpretable check.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::stats::MDS model(dm::datasets::iris(),
      {"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"},{4});
  std::print("{}\n",model.dimension_diagnostics().to_md(7));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| dimensions | gof_positive | gof_absolute | strain_squared | relative_strain | distance_rmse | relative_distance_error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.9246187 | 0.9246187 | 1455.8068078 | 0.0604520 | 0.3307987 | 0.1093831 |
| 2 | 0.9776852 | 0.9776852 | 148.4100785 | 0.0193015 | 0.1264017 | 0.0417964 |
| 3 | 0.9947878 | 0.9947878 | 12.6126469 | 0.0056268 | 0.0371897 | 0.0122973 |
| 4 | 1.0000000 | 1.0000000 | 0.0000000 | 0.0000000 | 0.0000000 | 0.0000000 |
// result_end
\caption(Euclidean iris diagnostics for one through four axes, from one fit. Four axes recover the original distances to numerical precision.)

The Shepard plots make the difference between metrics visible for all \(\binom{150}{2}=11175\)
unordered pairs. Euclidean two-axis distances lie below the equality line because an
orthogonal projection cannot increase distance. With Manhattan input, the negative
spectrum changes that geometry and some embedded distances can exceed the input values.

```{cpp, file="figures/mds_iris_shepard.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> features{"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  const dm::stats::MDS euclidean(iris,features), manhattan(iris,features,{2,dm::stats::DistanceMetric::Manhattan});
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  layout.add(euclidean.plot_shepard().title("Euclidean, two axes"))
        .add(manhattan.plot_shepard().title("Manhattan, two axes"));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/mds_iris_shepard.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/mds_iris_shepard.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All 11175 pair distances under each metric, compared with the corresponding two-axis embedding. Marker overlap is substantial; the cloud is a geometric residual check, not a frequency estimate.)
\alttext(Two dense blue distance clouds follow grey equality lines. The Euclidean cloud lies on or below its line, especially falling below it at short distances. The Manhattan cloud spreads on both sides at short and intermediate dissimilarities.)

>> Check PCA through invariants, not a correlation of chosen axes

The final check explicitly disables PCA scaling. The printed eigenvalue table tests
\(\lambda_j^{\mathrm{MDS}}=149\lambda_j^{\mathrm{PCA}}\).
The full reconstructed Gram matrices should agree as well; this check tolerates sign flips
and rotations in repeated-eigenvalue subspaces. A default standardised PCA fit would
instead analyse a different set of distances.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> features{"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  const dm::stats::MDS mds(iris,features,{4});
  const dm::stats::PCA pca(iris,features,{true,false});
  const auto a=mds.embedding()*mds.embedding().transpose(), b=pca.scores()*pca.scores().transpose();
  std::print("Relative Gram discrepancy: {:.12f}.\n\n",(a-b).norm_frobenius()/b.norm_frobenius());
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  std::vector<double> dimension,mds_eigen,pca_eigen,error;
  for (std::size_t j=0;j<4;++j) {
    dimension.push_back(j+1); mds_eigen.push_back(mds.eigenvalues()[j]);
    pca_eigen.push_back((iris.nrows()-1)*pca.explained_variance()[j]);
    error.push_back(std::abs(mds_eigen.back()-pca_eigen.back()));
  }
  table.add_column("axis",dimension); table.add_column("MDS eigenvalue",mds_eigen);
  table.add_column("149 times PCA eigenvalue",pca_eigen); table.add_column("absolute error",error);
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(10));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Relative Gram discrepancy: 0.000000000000.

| axis | MDS eigenvalue | 149 times PCA eigenvalue | absolute error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 630.00801420 | 630.00801420 | 0 |
| 2 | 36.15794144 | 36.15794144 | 0 |
| 3 | 11.65321551 | 11.65321551 | 0 |
| 4 | 3.55142885 | 3.55142885 | 0 |
// result_end
\caption(The Euclidean-MDS/PCA equivalence checked at the spectrum and reconstructed-Gram levels. This establishes the same centred geometry without requiring identical coordinates.)

The displayed distances, full spectra, reconstructed Gram matrices and goodness-of-fit
fractions were checked independently with R 4.5.2 `cmdscale` (without an additive correction),
and the Euclidean geometry with `prcomp(center=TRUE,scale.=FALSE)`. NumPy independently
reconstructs the matrices and all prefix residuals from exported inputs and coordinates.
All 101 cycle spectra also match the analytic eigenvalues. These checks compare geometric
invariants; no coordinate sign is chosen merely to make two plots look alike.

> Conclusion

The rectangle exposes every step between distances and coordinates: squaring, removing
means, diagonalising a Gram matrix and dropping an axis. Its retained inertia, strain and
distance loss are three different quantities with different values. The cycle then shows
why a perfect positive-inertia fraction can coexist with unavoidable distortion, and supplies
a concrete configuration with smaller distance loss than the classical solution. The
spectrum sweep locates exactly where the obstruction appears.

Iris behaves as the Euclidean derivation predicts: its classical map is unscaled PCA in
another orientation, and its four-feature distances require at most four axes. Manhattan
input poses a different geometry, so negative eigenvalues and direct residuals must be
examined alongside retained positive inertia. A useful two-dimensional picture is evidence
about the chosen dissimilarities; its appearance alone is neither a proof of exact distance
recovery nor a validation of a predictive model.

> References

- W. S. Torgerson (1952). *Multidimensional scaling: I. Theory and method*. Psychometrika 17, 401–419.
  [Original paper|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02288916].
- J. C. Gower (1966). *Some distance properties of latent root and vector methods used in multivariate analysis*.
  Biometrika 53, 325–338. [Original paper|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/53.3-4.325].
- R Core Team. *Classical (Metric) Multidimensional Scaling*.
  [stats::cmdscale documentation|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cmdscale.html].
  Definitions of the two spectral goodness-of-fit fractions; datamunge keeps the requested
  number of columns and does not implement the optional Cailliez additive correction.
